<h1>How warmer waters may be fueling stronger Florida hurricanes</h1>
<p class="byline">By Georgia Ruffolo</p>

<p>There are few places in the world that know hurricanes like Florida. With more than 1,200 miles of coastline along the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico, the state often finds itself in the midst of these storms, and every stretch of its coast has been hit by at least one major storm since 1850. But over the last couple of decades, the story has shifted from simply “how frequent do storms hit?” to “how ferocious — and how costly — are they when they do?”</p>

<p>Since 1980, Florida has faced about 41% of all U.S. hurricanes and an estimated 450 billion dollars in cumulative disaster costs, leading all fifty states, according to a compilation of state hurricane impacts based on NOAA and Florida Climate Center data.<sup><a href="https://www.mesocore.com/blog/florida-hurricane-impact-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Hurricanes have become some of the most expensive disasters in American history; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Ian" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hurricane Ian</a>, which struck southwest Florida in 2022, is now considered the costliest hurricane in Florida’s history, with estimated losses around 112 billion dollars.<sup><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Ian" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup> Those price tags matter for homeowners, local governments, and insurers, but they also suggest a deeper question: what, exactly, is making some of these storms so intense?</p>

<p>One place scientists increasingly look for answers is just offshore, in the water itself. Sea surface temperatures — the temperature of the top layers of the ocean — have been climbing for decades. In South Florida, <a href="https://southeastfloridaclimatecompact.org/initiative/climate-indicators-sea-surface-temperature/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a regional climate indicator based on NOAA’s long‑term sea surface temperature record</a> shows that summertime ocean temperatures around the tip of the peninsula have warmed by about 0.49 °F per decade from 1982 to 2024. That may sound pretty small, but over multiple decades, it adds up to a noticeably warmer foundation for any storm moving over the region.</p>

<h2>Florida’s hurricane pattern, in context</h2>

<p>To understand how Florida’s hurricanes have changed, I visited NOAA’s official hurricane database, often called HURDAT2, and <a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/hrd/hurdat/All_U.S._Hurricanes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a summary of “Continental United States Hurricane Impacts/Landfalls”</a> that lists which storms struck each state. These records track storm names, dates, paths, maximum sustained winds, and Saffir–Simpson category at landfall, which allows for a clean comparison of how many hurricanes hit Florida each year and how strong they were when they did.</p>

<p>Looking across the last twenty‑plus years, the pattern is uneven, but telling. Florida has seen very active periods — such as the mid‑2000s, when multiple hurricanes made landfall in back‑to‑back seasons — and calmer stretches with fewer direct hits but still damaging storms. The big picture from recent climate research, though, is that while the number of tropical cyclones varies quite a bit from year to year, a larger share of the storms that do form are now reaching major hurricane strength (Category 3 or higher). In other words, intensity is trending up, even when storm counts bounce around.</p>

<p>For Floridians, that matters far more than abstract global averages. On the ground, higher‑category landfalls translate into stronger winds, denser storm surge, and a bigger gap between what homes and infrastructure were designed to handle and what the storm actually delivers.</p>

