<h2>What’s happening in the water?</h2>

<p>The ocean side of this story leans on NOAA’s Optimum Interpolation Sea Surface Temperature (OISST) dataset, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/optimum-interpolation-sst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a long‑term climate record that blends satellite, ship, buoy, and float observations</a> into a consistent daily map of global sea surface temperatures. For South Florida, <a href="https://southeastfloridaclimatecompact.org/initiative/climate-indicators-sea-surface-temperature/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">a regional indicator</a> analyzes the summertime (July–September) average SST for waters surrounding the tip of the peninsula, which gives a simple way to track how coastal temperatures have evolved.</p>

<p>From 1982 to 2024, that indicator shows a clear upward trend. Summertime SST in the South Florida region has warmed nearly half a degree Fahrenheit per decade. The same source notes that this ocean trend closely matches the rise in nighttime minimum air temperatures over land — about 0.52 °F per decade — suggesting that the atmosphere and ocean in the region are warming together. So, for you Floridians out there battling the heat, you should know that this isn’t just a hotter‑than‑normal summer. It’s a slow, steady, and serious shift in Florida’s natural environment.</p>

<p>Globally, <a href="https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/ocean/sea-surface-temperature" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA’s sea surface temperature overview</a> puts typical SSTs in the tropics at more than 86 °F, compared with temperatures near 28 °F close to the poles. Warmer water in the tropics and subtropics is one of the reasons those regions host the world’s most powerful tropical cyclones. Classic work on hurricane formation showed that storms generally need sea surface temperatures above roughly 26–27 °C (around 79–81 °F) to form and become stronger.<sup><a href="https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/clim/10/11/1520-0442_1997_010_2921_trbsst_2.0.co_2.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4</a></sup> South Florida’s coastal waters routinely hit and surpass that threshold during summer and early fall, which means any extra warming adds onto an already favorable setting for storms.</p>

