<h2>How warmer waters can feed stronger storms</h2>

<p>Physically, the connection between warmer oceans and stronger hurricanes comes down to energy. Hurricanes draw power from latent heat — energy released when warm, moist air rises from the ocean surface and condenses into clouds. The warmer the ocean surface and upper layer are, the more energy is available to fuel that process. A recent explainer on Atlantic hurricanes notes that sea surface temperatures above 26.5 °C, down to about 50 meters depth, can support a storm’s intensification into a hurricane, and that episodes of extremely high SST — marine heatwaves — have made rapid intensification about 50 percent more likely.<sup><a href="https://www.carbonbrief.org/explainer-how-hotter-oceans-can-fuel-more-intense-atlantic-hurricanes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6</a></sup></p>

<p>Researchers have also tried to quantify this link more directly. One correlation analysis of sea surface temperature and storm intensity found a positive relationship between higher SST at the time of hurricane events and stronger storms by using regression methods along storm tracks.<sup><a href="https://digitalcommons.murraystate.edu/scholarsweek/Fall2023/EES/6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7</a></sup> Another modeling study that looked at future climate scenarios concluded that projected increases in average SST are likely to lead to more intense hurricanes by the end of the twenty‑first century.<sup><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42452-023-05423-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8</a></sup> These studies don’t claim that sea surface temperature explains everything, but they do treat warmer water as a consistent pressure pushing storms toward higher categories.</p>

<p>In Florida, recent storm history offers the best examples. Hurricane Ian in 2022 became the costliest hurricane in the state’s history, with estimated losses around 112 billion dollars, and made landfall near Cayo Costa as a high‑end Category 4 storm.<sup><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Ian" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup> Scientists highlighted unusually warm waters in the eastern Gulf of Mexico as one of the reasons behind Ian’s rapid intensification before making landfall.<sup><a href="https://www.carbonbrief.org/explainer-how-hotter-oceans-can-fuel-more-intense-atlantic-hurricanes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6</a></sup> Other recent storms, including Michael in 2018 and Helene in 2024, also intensified over extremely warm waters before hitting the state.<sup><a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/hrd/hurdat/All_U.S._Hurricanes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup> Taken together, these cases reinforce what the data suggest: when coastal waters are anomalously hot during peak hurricane season, storms moving over them become stronger.</p>

