<h2>What the research can — and can’t — say</h2>

<p>Even with solid datasets, there are limits to what researchers can deem as certain. Sea surface temperature is only one piece of the hurricane puzzle. Studies that combine SST with other metrics, like sea surface height anomalies, suggest that the depth and structure of the warm layer beneath the surface can be just as important as the top layer of the ocean.<sup><a href="https://ams.confex.com/ams/pdfpapers/151831.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">9</a></sup> Wind shear, atmospheric moisture, storm size and track, and local coastal geography are all essential in shaping how a hurricane evolves and what kind of damage it leaves behind.</p>

<p>On the impact side, this story focuses on storm severity mainly through wind‑based categories because that’s what HURDAT2 tracks most precisely. But rainfall, storm surge, and tornado outbreaks can drastically change how destructive a storm is without necessarily showing up in the category number. For instance, a slow‑moving Category 2 that stalls and floods an entire metro area can cause significantly more damage than a fast‑moving Category 3 that passes through quickly.</p>

<p>Most importantly, correlation does not equal causation. Even if the scatter plot shows that years with warmer South Florida waters tend to coincide with stronger Florida hurricanes, that pattern will still represent just one contributing factor among many. The point here is not to say that sea surface temperature alone “creates” any specific storm, but to show that the ocean is silently raising the baseline temperature for every hurricane season. For a state built along the water, understanding that shift — and planning for storms that are more severe — is becoming just as important as tracking the next system on the radar.</p>

